baul-window-manage-views: Fix swapped lists in handle_go_forward

The "Move extra links from Forward to Back list" loop removed the
bookmark from the back list and prepended it to the forward list,
exactly the opposite of what it must do (compare with the symmetric
loop in handle_go_back).

When jumping forward more than one step at once (Forward dropdown
menu, distance >= 1), g_list_remove() fails to find the bookmark in
the wrong list and returns that list unchanged, so both list heads
end up aliasing the same chain: forward entries silently vanish,
bookmarks leak, the jump lands on the wrong location, and one entry
ends up listed in both chains while holding a single reference.
Clearing the forward chain on the next navigation unrefs that entry
while the back chain still points at it, leaving a dangling pointer
behind.

Steps to reproduce:

  1. Open baul and navigate to 4 different folders (A -> B -> C -> D)
  2. Press Back twice (you end up in B; Forward now holds C and D)
  3. Open the Forward button dropdown and choose the second entry
     (a 2-step jump): this corrupts the back/forward chains
  4. Navigate to any new folder
  5. Press Back (or open the Back dropdown): the dangling pointer
     can take baul down

Observed in practice: the window freezes and becomes unusable rather
than crashing outright (use-after-free is undefined behavior; with
gdb --args baul it is clearly visible, without a debugger it can go
unnoticed or just show up as odd behavior in the menus).

Swap the two lists so the loop really moves links from the forward
list to the back list.
